#!/usr/bin/bash
# The WRITE half of the Steam client's OS Update Channel picker:
#   /usr/bin/steamos-polkit-helpers/steamos-select-branch <branch>
#
# Confirmed in the baked seed's steamclient.so (not steamui.so — see novadeck-select-branch's
# header). CSystemManagerSelectOSBranchJob calls this ABSOLUTE path, while the two read jobs
# (-l, -c) call the bare `steamos-select-branch` off PATH. That asymmetry is the only reason this
# file exists next to the symlink in /usr/bin: the client asks for the selection in a different
# place than it asks for the list.
#
# THIS WRAPPER IS NOT A PRIVILEGE BOUNDARY, exactly as its steamos-update sibling is not. On
# SteamOS this directory holds pkexec wrappers from jupiter-hw-support, which we do not bake, so
# the path is only a path. Nothing here needs privilege anyway: the selection is written to
# /home/deck/.config/novadeck/ota.conf, which the session user owns. That is deliberate rather
# than convenient — it is the one channel source reachable on a release device, where there is no
# sudo and root's authorized_keys is never written.
#
# THE JOURNAL LINE IS NOT HERE, unlike the steamos-update wrapper, and that placement is the point.
# This wrapper is on the WRITE path only; -l and -c reach the implementation through the /usr/bin
# symlink and never run this script. Logging here alone left `journalctl -t steamos-select-branch`
# EMPTY on a device whose dropdown was populated — the two calls the picker depends on were the
# unlogged ones. novadeck-select-branch logs every invocation itself, which covers all three paths;
# a second logger call here would only double the write path's lines.
exec /usr/bin/novadeck-select-branch "$@"
